WEBVTT

1
00:00:14.015 --> 00:00:19.059
cannot begin to describe my pain and embarrassment at being forced to stand on that stool.

2
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:25.824
Just as I thought I couldn't endure another moment, a girl passed me and looked up kindly.

3
00:00:27.085 --> 00:00:31.668
After that, I managed to overcome my rising hysteria and stand up straight.

4
00:00:32.872 --> 00:00:36.593
Helen also passed by and gave me an encouraging smile.

5
00:00:37.833 --> 00:00:43.835
Scarcely an hour before, I'd heard Miss Scatcherd condemn her to a meal of bread and water for

6
00:00:43.875 --> 00:00:45.776
sloppily copying an exercise.

7
00:00:47.036 --> 00:00:50.297
Now she wore a badge on her arm that said "Untidy."

8
00:00:51.517 --> 00:00:57.019
Before the half hour ended, classes were dismissed, and everyone went to the dining hall for tea.

9
00:00:58.179 --> 00:01:00.300
It was dusk when I descended from the stool.

10
00:01:01.472 --> 00:01:05.195
The spell that had allowed me to repress my feelings began to dissolve.

11
00:01:06.436 --> 00:01:09.819
Overwhelmed by grief, I lay on the floor and wept.

12
00:01:11.581 --> 00:01:16.485
I had meant to be so good and to do so much at Lowood: to make many friends,

13
00:01:16.805 --> 00:01:18.827
to earn respect, and to win affection.

14
00:01:19.948 --> 00:01:25.152
That very morning Miss Temple had promised to teach me drawing and to let me learn French if

15
00:01:25.232 --> 00:01:26.473
I continued to improve.

16
00:01:27.654 --> 00:01:29.676
I was well liked by my fellow students.

17
00:01:30.864 --> 00:01:31.925
Now I was crushed.

18
00:01:33.046 --> 00:01:36.089
I heard someone approaching; I looked up and saw Helen.

19
00:01:36.109 --> 00:01:41.093
"Come, eat something," she said, offering me coffee and bread.

20
00:01:42.174 --> 00:01:43.555
But I pushed everything away.

21
00:01:44.756 --> 00:01:48.399
Regarding me with surprise, Helen sat next to me on the floor.

22
00:01:48.419 --> 00:01:53.583
"Helen, why do you stay with a girl whom everybody believes to be a liar?"

23
00:01:54.003 --> 00:01:54.484
I asked.

24
00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:01.324
"There are only eighty people who have heard you called so, Jane, and the world contains millions of

25
00:02:01.384 --> 00:02:04.987
people." "Who cares about millions?"

26
00:02:05.427 --> 00:02:06.648
I asked inconsolably.

27
00:02:06.668 --> 00:02:09.950
"The eighty girls here now despise me."

28
00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:13.933
"Probably no one here despises or dislikes you.

29
00:02:15.094 --> 00:02:16.435
I'm sure many pity you."

30
00:02:16.455 --> 00:02:19.397
"How can they pity me after what Mr.

31
00:02:19.437 --> 00:02:20.337
Brocklehurst said?"

32
00:02:20.357 --> 00:02:23.159
"Oh, no one likes Mr.

33
00:02:23.179 --> 00:02:26.333
Brocklehurst!" Helen reached for my hand.

34
00:02:27.494 --> 00:02:32.356
"Teachers and students may look coldly on you for a day or two, but they'll be friendly again."

35
00:02:33.497 --> 00:02:36.259
Helen had calmed me, but I still felt sad.

36
00:02:37.459 --> 00:02:39.140
I rested my head on her shoulder.

37
00:02:40.241 --> 00:02:43.823
We had not been sitting long like that when Miss Temple entered the room.

38
00:02:43.843 --> 00:02:47.525
"I came to find you, Jane," said Miss Temple.

39
00:02:47.545 --> 00:02:51.528
"I want you to go to my room, and Helen may go too."

40
00:02:52.648 --> 00:02:58.891
In her cheerful apartment, Miss Temple directed Helen to sit in an armchair next to the fire while

41
00:02:58.931 --> 00:03:01.413
she took another chair and called me to her side.

42
00:03:01.433 --> 00:03:04.875
"Have you cried your grief away?"

43
00:03:05.235 --> 00:03:06.235
Miss Temple asked me.

44
00:03:06.255 --> 00:03:08.737
"I'm afraid I'll never do that."

45
00:03:08.757 --> 00:03:14.800
"Why?" "Because I've been wrongly accused, and everyone now thinks me wicked."

46
00:03:15.981 --> 00:03:17.501
Miss Temple patted my shoulder.

47
00:03:17.522 --> 00:03:21.764
"Continue to act as a good girl, and you'll satisfy me.

48
00:03:23.068 --> 00:03:26.490
Now defend yourself to me without exaggerating anything."

49
00:03:27.631 --> 00:03:30.952
And so I told her the story of my sad childhood.

50
00:03:32.033 --> 00:03:35.115
In the course of the tale, I happened to mention Mr.

51
00:03:35.155 --> 00:03:36.415
Lloyd, the apothecary.

52
00:03:37.636 --> 00:03:42.859
Miss Temple vowed to write to him, and if he agreed with my story, then she would clear

53
00:03:42.899 --> 00:03:43.559
me of lying.

54
00:03:43.579 --> 00:03:50.063
"To me, Jane, you are clear now," she said, and then she asked after Helen's cough.

55
00:03:50.083 --> 00:03:52.424
"And the pain in your chest?"

56
00:03:53.544 --> 00:03:55.345
"A little better," responded Helen.

57
00:03:57.106 --> 00:03:59.347
Miss Temple got up and felt Helen's pulse.

58
00:04:00.547 --> 00:04:02.588
Returning to her seat, she sighed.

59
00:04:03.809 --> 00:04:06.390
She rang for a servant to bring tea and toast.

60
00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:09.931
How fragrant was the scent of that toast!

61
00:04:11.052 --> 00:04:12.893
But there didn't seem to be enough for three.

62
00:04:12.913 --> 00:04:18.435
"Barbara," Miss Temple said to the servant, "can you bring us some more toast?"

63
00:04:19.635 --> 00:04:25.753
Barbara went out and soon returned with the message that the housekeeper had sent up the usual amount.

64
00:04:25.773 --> 00:04:29.835
"We'll have to make do then," said Miss Temple.

65
00:04:30.875 --> 00:04:33.215
But when Barbara had left, she smiled.

66
00:04:33.235 --> 00:04:38.057
"For once I have it in my power to make up for a lack of food."

67
00:04:39.277 --> 00:04:42.798
She went to a drawer and pulled out a parcel wrapped in paper.

68
00:04:43.938 --> 00:04:46.239
Inside was a seedcake!

69
00:04:47.459 --> 00:04:51.998
After we'd had tea and cake, she sent us on our way with a tender embrace.

70
00:04:53.179 --> 00:04:54.741
"God bless you, my children!"

71
00:04:56.442 --> 00:05:00.305
When we arrived in our bedroom, Miss Scatcherd was examining drawers.

72
00:05:00.325 --> 00:05:03.728
"Your things are in shameful disorder!"

73
00:05:04.128 --> 00:05:05.049
she said to Helen.

74
00:05:05.069 --> 00:05:07.812
"I know," replied Helen.

75
00:05:07.832 --> 00:05:11.254
"I intended to arrange them, but I forgot."

76
00:05:12.456 --> 00:05:17.540
The next morning Miss Scatcherd wrote the word "Slob" in large letters on a piece of cardboard.

77
00:05:18.728 --> 00:05:20.929
Then she tied the sign to Helen's head.

78
00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:24.851
My friend, ever patient, wore it until evening.

79
00:05:25.992 --> 00:05:30.835
But the moment Miss Scatcherd was finished teaching for the day, I threw the sign in the fire.

80
00:05:32.055 --> 00:05:34.197
A week later a letter arrived from Mr.

81
00:05:34.217 --> 00:05:39.459
Lloyd, and Miss Temple assembled the whole school to say that I'd been cleared of lying.

82
00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:47.344
I was allowed to begin drawing and French lessons, and I now preferred Lowood with all its deprivations

83
00:05:47.724 --> 00:05:49.345
to Gateshead and its luxuries.

84
00:05:51.346 --> 00:05:55.128
Spring came to the school, and the grounds grew green and flowery.

85
00:05:56.309 --> 00:05:58.690
But with spring came an outbreak of typhus.

86
00:05:59.931 --> 00:06:02.212
Forty-five girls lay sick at one time.

87
00:06:03.473 --> 00:06:04.853
Many went home to die.

88
00:06:05.954 --> 00:06:09.896
There were no classes and few rules for those of us who stayed healthy.

89
00:06:11.097 --> 00:06:16.920
I had not seen Helen for a long time because she was ill with another disease, tuberculosis.

90
00:06:18.328 --> 00:06:21.469
One evening in June, I stayed outdoors, wandering the grounds.

91
00:06:22.709 --> 00:06:27.230
We'd heard rumors that someone was very ill and that the doctor had been sent for.

92
00:06:28.511 --> 00:06:33.012
As I was coming in, a nurse opened the door for the doctor, who was just leaving.

93
00:06:33.032 --> 00:06:34.792
"How's Helen?"

94
00:06:35.153 --> 00:06:35.933
I asked the nurse.

95
00:06:35.953 --> 00:06:38.413
"Very poorly.

96
00:06:39.554 --> 00:06:41.874
The doctor says she has not long to live."

97
00:06:43.055 --> 00:06:44.915
Horror rippled through me!

98
00:06:44.935 --> 00:06:46.776
"Where is she?"

99
00:06:47.136 --> 00:06:47.936
I asked the nurse.

100
00:06:49.088 --> 00:06:52.210
"In Miss Temple's room, but no one is allowed to see her.

101
00:06:53.351 --> 00:06:53.711
Come in.

102
00:06:54.872 --> 00:06:56.513
It's time for everyone to be in bed."

103
00:06:58.374 --> 00:07:01.957
I went to bed but was much too full of worry and grief to sleep.

104
00:07:03.238 --> 00:07:07.821
Around eleven o'clock I crept up to Miss Temple's room and found Helen in bed.

105
00:07:07.841 --> 00:07:10.583
"Helen, are you awake?"

106
00:07:10.963 --> 00:07:11.543
I whispered.

107
00:07:12.664 --> 00:07:17.908
She was pale, but otherwise seemed so unchanged that I couldn't believe she was going to die.

108
00:07:19.112 --> 00:07:20.293
Helen opened her eyes.

109
00:07:20.313 --> 00:07:22.554
"Can it be you, Jane?

110
00:07:23.635 --> 00:07:24.475
Why are you here?"

111
00:07:24.495 --> 00:07:28.477
"I heard you were very ill, and I had to speak to you."

112
00:07:29.798 --> 00:07:31.699
"So you came to bid me good-bye."

113
00:07:31.719 --> 00:07:33.700
"No, Helen!"

114
00:07:34.961 --> 00:07:39.243
As I tried to hide my tears, a coughing fit seized my dear friend.

115
00:07:40.444 --> 00:07:45.487
We talked quietly, and then I sat with her, holding her hand until she fell asleep.

116
00:07:46.647 --> 00:07:49.785
The next morning I learned that Helen had died.
